 "findings": [
  "EVERY REGION MAPS TO A TRADITION, so need and scarcity DERIVED rather than got invented: a people needs what its craft cannot make and has a surplus of what its craft produces. Same generative discipline as the crafts, the villainy and the moneys.",
  "⚠️ THE DEAD LISTS WROTE THEMSELVES AND THEY ARE THE BEST PART. `mech_parts`/`precursor_salvage`/`nanite_tech` are worth ZERO in the Quickwood (Erik's own example, now authored). `worked_light` is worthless in the Umbral Depths — and trying to sell it there reads as an insult, the only socially DANGEROUS dead entry. `arms` are a hard zero in the Stillhold, consistent with the tradition refusing to honour a Marcher oath. `documents` are worthless in the Unspooling — you cannot sell a ledger to the Churn. And `luxuries` are zero in the Unmade: a people whose craft is careful ending does not buy keepsakes.",
  "THE CROSSING IS THE ONLY REGION WITH AN EMPTY DEAD LIST, and that IS its identity expressed mechanically — the one place in the world that wants everything. It should also never pay BEST for anything, so selling only at the Crossing costs you margin but never strands you.",
  "⚠️ TWO ENTRIES PROVE THE TWO-AXIS MODEL WAS NECESSARY: cut_stone in the Open Reach is `little` need and `absent` scarcity — MAXIMALLY RARE AND NEARLY WORTHLESS, because a travelling people does not buy building material. Under my old one-axis model that would have priced at ×2.5. It prices at ×0.75.",
  "THE BEST HONEST TRADE IN THE WORLD IS ROOTKIN SEED CARRIED TO THE PALELANDS: high need, absent scarcity, ×5.0 — the top of the range. And it is a GOOD trade in every sense: you are bringing life to a death-steeped land that will pay anything for it.",
  "AND THE ECONOMY AGREES WITH THE VILLAINY PASS WITHOUT COORDINATION: seraphic charters are `abundant` and cheap in the Ascent and needed everywhere else, WHICH IS EXACTLY WHY THE LICENCE TRADE IS PROFITABLE. The Veiled Reach buys real documents at high need — the name-takers' supply chain, priced."
 ]
